{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011CdQqK24cHqqFt9biBKGFR","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"BQN doesn't have a hook train — that's the first thing worth pinning down. In BQN the 2-train is an **Atop**, not a hook (unlike J, where `F G` is a hook). Hooks in BQN are written with the combinators `⊸` and `⟜`. So there are two mechanisms to cover: trains (Atop/Fork) and the hook modifiers.\n\n## Fork — the 3-train\n\nThree functions juxtaposed. The outer two each get the arguments, and the middle one combines their results:\n\n```bqn\n(F G H) 𝕩    →    (F 𝕩) G (H 𝕩)\n𝕨 (F G H) 𝕩  →  (𝕨 F 𝕩) G (𝕨 F… 𝕩)   ⍝ i.e. (𝕨 F 𝕩) G (𝕨 H 𝕩)\n```\n\nThe classic:\n\n```bqn\n    Mean ← +´ ÷ ≠\n    Mean 1‿2‿3‿4      ⍝ (+´𝕩) ÷ (≠𝕩)\n2.5\n\n    SortDown ← ⍒⊏⊢    ⍝ (⍒𝕩) ⊏ (⊢𝕩)\n    SortDown 3‿1‿4‿1‿5\n⟨ 5 4 3 1 1 ⟩\n```\n\nModifiers bind tighter than trains, so `+´÷≠` parses as `(+´) ÷ (≠)`, not as anything involving `´` applied to a train.\n\nA **subject** is allowed in the left tine, where it acts as a constant:\n\n```bqn\n    (0⌈⊢) ¯2‿3‿¯4     ⍝ 0 ⌈ 𝕩 — clamp negatives to zero\n⟨ 0 3 0 ⟩\n```\n\n## Atop — the 2-train\n\nTwo functions juxtaposed. The left one is always applied **monadically** to the result of the right one, which receives both arguments:\n\n```bqn\n(F G) 𝕩    →   F (  G 𝕩)\n𝕨 (F G) 𝕩  →   F (𝕨 G 𝕩)\n```\n\n```bqn\n    5 (|-) 8          ⍝ | (5 - 8) — absolute difference\n3\n    (∧´ ⌽⊸=) \"abcba\"  ⍝ ∧´ applied to the result of ⌽⊸=\n1\n```\n\nThis is identical to the `∘` combinator: `F G` ≡ `F∘G`. The train form just saves the glyph.\n\n## Longer trains\n\nGroup the **rightmost three** into a fork, repeatedly, until 2 or 1 remain:\n\n```bqn\nA B C D E   →   A B (C D E)      ⍝ fork whose right tine is a fork\nA B C D     →   A (B C D)        ⍝ atop of a fork\n```\n\nTo override that grouping, parenthesize, or use Nothing (`·`) as a left tine — a fork with `·` on the left collapses to an Atop, which lets you insert an atop mid-train.\n\n## Hooks: `⊸` and `⟜`\n\nThese are 2-modifiers, not trains. They're the \"hook\" shapes:\n\n```bqn\n(F⊸G) 𝕩    →   (F 𝕩) G 𝕩        ⍝ Before — F preprocesses the left side\n𝕨 (F⊸G) 𝕩  →   (F 𝕨) G 𝕩\n\n(F⟜G) 𝕩    →   𝕩 F (G 𝕩)        ⍝ After — G preprocesses the right side\n𝕨 (F⟜G) 𝕩  →   𝕨 F (G 𝕩)\n```\n\n`F⟜G` monadically is J's hook. Either operand may be a subject, which is the everyday use — that's what `2⊸×`, `-⟜1`, `0⊸<` are:\n\n```bqn\n    (-⟜1) 5           ⍝ 5 - 1\n4\n    Center ← -⟜(+´÷≠) ⍝ 𝕩 - Mean 𝕩\n    Center 1‿2‿3\n⟨ ¯1 0 1 ⟩\n    (⌽⊸≡) \"racecar\"   ⍝ (⌽𝕩) ≡ 𝕩\n1\n```\n\n## How hooks relate to trains\n\nMonadically the hooks are expressible as forks with `⊢`; **dyadically they differ**, and that difference is the whole reason both exist:\n\n| | monadic | dyadic |\n|---|---|---|\n| `F⊸G` | `(F 𝕩) G 𝕩` | `(F 𝕨) G 𝕩` |\n| fork `F G ⊢` | `(F 𝕩) G 𝕩` | `(𝕨 F 𝕩) G 𝕩` |\n| `F⟜G` | `𝕩 F (G 𝕩)` | `𝕨 F (G 𝕩)` |\n| fork `⊢ F G` | `𝕩 F (G 𝕩)` | `𝕨 F (𝕨 G 𝕩)` |\n\nIn the hook forms, each argument reaches exactly one operand. In the fork forms, both arguments are fed to both tines. Reach for `⊸`/`⟜` when you want to transform one argument on the way in; reach for a fork when both tines genuinely need to see the same data.\n\nOne naming note if you're coming from Dyalog: BQN's assignment rules mean a train bound to a name needs an uppercase first letter (`Mean ← +´÷≠`), since lowercase names are subjects and would trigger a value-vs-function mismatch."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2358,"cache_read_input_tokens":5058,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2358},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"284fb311-0d8d-41f5-9bba-12c6a4c6fa82","uuid":"b55f11f9-00b1-4637-86e5-d4627df35443","timestamp